From f95431421580de984d1be8adae8600fbe8437a52 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 16:35:32 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 101/104] packetizer: a52: let a break cost one frame, not the
 rest of the block

The packetizer takes one frame per call and hands what is left of the block
back to its caller, which offers it again on the next call. A block that
arrived carrying a discontinuity therefore arrives carrying it every time, and
each time the parser is flushed. The second flush finds the block's timestamp
already spent - it is cleared once used, so that no two frames claim it - so
the frame is sent undated and dropped, and the rest of the block goes with it
because nothing dates the head of the bytestream again until a later block
reaches it. Each flush also arms b_discontuinity again, so a second output
frame is stamped and the decoder acts on the same seam twice.

Measured on a four second a/52 item looped three times with --input-repeat on
the dummy sink, frames handed to the output per pass:

    ts, ffmpeg's default pes     122/122/123  ->  125/125/126 frames
    ts, 16 kB pes payload        114 per pass ->  125 per pass

and with the larger pes - about twenty frames to a block, which is what a
broadcast multiplex looks like - the 352 ms lost at every loop was enough to
starve the output: "ran out 3 time(s), worst shortfall 1739113 us" becomes
"ran out 0 time(s)". Output frames stamped DISCONTINUITY fall from two per
seam to one, and flushes per seam from three to two.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 modules/packetizer/a52.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/modules/packetizer/a52.c b/modules/packetizer/a52.c
index a637ec9a77..6f84f3d676 100644
--- a/modules/packetizer/a52.c
+++ b/modules/packetizer/a52.c
@@ -169,6 +169,16 @@ static block_t *PacketizeBlock( decoder_t *p_dec, block_t **pp_block )
                 block_Release( p_block );
                 return NULL;
             }
+
+            /* What is left of this block once a frame has been taken out of
+             * it comes straight back here, and the break belongs to the seam
+             * the block arrived on, not to the rest of the block: leaving the
+             * flag set makes every following frame flush the parser again,
+             * and the second flush arrives with the block's timestamp already
+             * spent, so the remainder of the block is dropped for want of one
+             * - and each flush arms the outbound flag again, stamping another
+             * frame the decoder then acts on. */
+            p_block->i_flags &= ~BLOCK_FLAG_DISCONTINUITY;
         }
 
         block_BytestreamPush( &p_sys->bytestream, p_block );
